## Why This Is Asked
Tests augmented BST design for duplicates and range queries on a dynamic multiset, focusing on balance, memory, and correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Treap with duplicates via per-node cnt
- Subtree size and sum augmentation
- kth smallest by counts; range queries via splits or traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node{constructor(key,prio){this.key=key;this.prio=prio;this.cnt=1;this.l=null;this.r=null;this.sz=1;this.sum=key}};
function upd(n){if(!n)return; n.sz=n.cnt+(n.l?.sz||0)+(n.r?.sz||0); n.sum=n.key*n.cnt+(n.l?.sum||0)+(n.r?.sum||0)}
function rotateRight(y){const x=y.l; y.l=x.r; x.r=y; upd(y); upd(x); return x}
function rotateLeft(x){const y=x.r; x.r=y.l; y.l=x; upd(x); upd(y); return y}
function insert(n, key){if(!n)return new Node(key,Math.random()); if(key===n.key){n.cnt++; upd(n);return n}; if(key<n.key){n.l=insert(n.l,key); if(n.l.prio>n.prio) n=rotateRight(n);} else {n.r=insert(n.r,key); if(n.r.prio>n.prio) n=rotateLeft(n);} upd(n); return n}
function kth(n,k){if(!n) return null; const leftSize=n.l?.sz||0; if(k<=leftSize) return kth(n.l,k); if(k>leftSize+n.cnt) return kth(n.r,k-leftSize-n.cnt); return n.key;}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests understanding of stack augmentation, a practical pattern for maintaining auxiliary state. It demonstrates the ability to design data structures that support efficient queries while handling edge cases like duplicates and empty states.

## Key Concepts
- Two auxiliary stacks (minStack and maxStack) track running minima and maxima in sync with the main stack
- Each push/pop operation updates all three stacks atomically, preserving O(1) time complexity
- Duplicates are handled naturally since the min/max is recalculated at each step
- Empty stack edge cases must be handled (typically by checking stack length before accessing top)

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinMaxStack {
  constructor() {
    this.stack = [];
    this.minStack = [];
    this.maxStack = [];
  }

  push(x) {
    this.stack.push(x);
    this.minStack.push(
      this.minStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
    this.maxStack.push(
      this.maxStack.length ? Math.max(x, this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) return undefined;
    this.minStack.pop();
    this.maxStack.pop();
    return this.stack.pop();
  }

  top() {
    return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];
  }

  getMin() {
    return this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1];
  }

  getMax() {
    return this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mapping of high-level operations to a classic data-structure primitive (Fenwick tree) with dynamic growth.

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree)
- 1-based indexing
- dynamic growth and delta updates
- handling duplicates in values

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.ft=new Array(n+1).fill(0); this.arr=[] }
  append(v){ this.arr.push(v); const i=this.arr.length; this.add(i,v); }
  add(i,delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+=i&-i) this.ft[i]+=delta; }
  update(i,v){ const delta=v-this.arr[i-1]; this.arr[i-1]=v; this.add(i,delta); }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-=i&-i) s+=this.ft[i]; return s; }
  range(l,r){ return this.sum(r)-this.sum(l-1); }
}
```

